WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.600
Lots of people die in "The Suicide Squad." DC gave&nbsp;
writer-director James Gunn blanket permission to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.040
kill any character he wanted, and he made good&nbsp;
use of that freedom. Here's a list of the major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.760
deaths in Gunn's gory romp.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.520
Blackguard, Captain Boomerang, TDK, Javelin,&nbsp;
Mongal, and Savant all die in the beach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.520
assault. This is the first version of&nbsp;
Task Force X assembled for the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.520
and they all die like jabronis in the&nbsp;
first 15 minutes. Blackguard sells his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.760
squadmates out to the hostiles, and his&nbsp;
reward is getting his own face blown off.&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:38.240
Almost everyone else on the beach dies from some&nbsp;
variation of being shot and/or blown up. Savant is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.800
an exception; his cranial bomb is detonated&nbsp;
by Amanda Waller after he tries to run away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.320
from the mission.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.600
During a press event Warner Bros. held for "The&nbsp;
Suicide Squad," actor Juan Diego Botto said,&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.760
"The character that I play, General&nbsp;
Silvio Luna, he is the new ruler of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.280
Corto Maltese island and he is obsessed with&nbsp;
two things: His collection of rainbow lorikeets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.160
and Harley Quinn. And those two obsessions&nbsp;
will determine his fate eventually."&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.960
He had to play it a little coy at the time with&nbsp;
the last bit, but we have no need for coyness now:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.920
Silvio Luna dies in "The Suicide Squad."&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.880
Silvio specifically seeks out Harley Quinn&nbsp;
and brings her to his palatial estate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.560
where he says he sees her as the embodiment&nbsp;
of American rebellion. Upon spending a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.600
glorious day with Harley, he proposes&nbsp;
marriage and lays out his plans for rule.&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.880
Unfortunately for him, mentioning that those&nbsp;
plans involve killing civilians and children is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.640
too much of a red flag. Harley shoots Silvio,&nbsp;
explaining that after her past relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.920
she just can't handle certain toxic elements.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.120
DC Comics character Ratcatcher's appearance&nbsp;
in "The Suicide Squad" is brief, and exists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.000
entirely in memory, but it's accurate to say&nbsp;
that his character, played by Taika Waititi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.560
dies in this movie. We learn his story during a&nbsp;
flashback: Cleo, aka Ratcatcher II, and her father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.520
grew up on the streets of Portugal, aided entirely&nbsp;
by his father's ability to communicate with rats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.400
through technology. The rats were able to provide&nbsp;
them with anything they needed. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:14.720
he was also a drug addict and died of an overdose.
This death led Chloe to take his rat wand and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.760
his name. She moved to America, but was sentenced&nbsp;
to Belle Reve for armed robbery after the rats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.280
were declared weapons. We only hear Ratcatcher&nbsp;
I speak roughly two sentences toward the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.000
the film in another flashback, but it's one of&nbsp;
the movie's most emotional moments, leaving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.560
audience with the idea that if something as&nbsp;
vile as a rat can find a place in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.160
anyone can.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.560
No death in "The Suicide Squad"&nbsp;
comes as a bigger shock than that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.634
of Rick Flag. He's the only non-Amanda Waller&nbsp;
character from the first "Suicide Squad" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:44.960
live past the opening credits. It's his nobility&nbsp;
that does him in: when he discovers the depth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:49.280
the US government's involvement with the&nbsp;
captivity of and research into Starro, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.320
decides to take a hard drive and tell the world.
Waller, however, has known about this all along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.440
and has a backup plan: Peacemaker. She gives&nbsp;
Peacemaker specific instructions to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:05.040
the information didn't leave the facility. Flag&nbsp;
and Peacemaker fight, and are for the most part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.160
evenly matched -- at least until Peacemaker&nbsp;
manages to fatally stab Flag in the heart. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:17.680
only unambiguous hero in Task Force X dies, but&nbsp;
not before alerting Peacemaker that Ratcatcher II&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.360
saw the whole thing.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.600
One of James Gunn's most significant&nbsp;
achievements in "The Suicide Squad"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.480
is making a tragic and sympathetic figure out&nbsp;
of Polka-Dot Man … and making the character's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.760
equally stupid death devastating. Abner Krill's&nbsp;
backstory is that his polka dot powers are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.680
result of an inter-dimensional infection, one set&nbsp;
up by his scientist mother. He resents his mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:45.760
so much that he sees every possible enemy&nbsp;
— that is to say, everyone — as her. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.480
self-conscious about his infection and his&nbsp;
powers, and just about begs for death.
 &nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.880
"We're all gonna die."
“I hope so."
 &nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.680
During the final battle with Starro, Bloodsport&nbsp;
has pointed instructions for Polka-Dot Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:03.760
pretend the giant starfish terrorizing the&nbsp;
city is his mother. Krill manages to inflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.840
massive damage with his polka-dots.
 
"He does throw polka-dots at people."
 &nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.200
But just when he's finally feeling confident about&nbsp;
his powers, his abilities, and his place in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.960
world, he gets crushed — literally.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:21.440
The Thinker is the foremost expert on Starro,&nbsp;
having conducted research on the giant alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.400
mind-controlling starfish for years. He makes&nbsp;
an uneasy alliance with the new regime on Corto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:32.800
Maltese after the coup. Both of these things lead&nbsp;
to this death, one inevitable as it is brutal.&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.920
Task Force X captures the Thinker and escorts&nbsp;
him to Jotunheim. While there, Task Force X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.520
sees Starro for the first time -- and, just&nbsp;
as damningly, all the people Starro controls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.120
The Thinker reveals Starro's origins, the research&nbsp;
he's spent decades conducting, and the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.880
the U.S. was in on it all along. After Jotunheim&nbsp;
explodes, Starro is freed -- and his first order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.920
of business is dealing with his captor. The&nbsp;
Thinker begs for his life, but it's no use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.600
as Starro rips the scientist limb from limb.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:10.960
Poor, poor Milton. He isn't a hero, he isn't a&nbsp;
villain, he isn't even an antihero. He's ... just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.560
some dude. He gets swept up with Task Force X and&nbsp;
drives them from Point A to Point B, albeit with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.760
more than a few unplanned stops between A and&nbsp;
B. He even helps with several of their schemes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.080
up to and including planting bombs at Jotunheim.
During a firefight on the upper floors of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.040
Jotunheim, Milton gets killed in the crossfire.&nbsp;
To add to the indignity of his death, some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.760
the team doesn't even know he's with them — they&nbsp;
assume he's back at the van. Harley, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.600
can't remember Milton's name — at least until she&nbsp;
sees the body. The perils of being just a normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.720
guy in a world of metahumans.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:50.000
General Mateo Suarez isn't just the right&nbsp;
hand man for Silvio Luna, he's the muscle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.720
A respected and feared military leader, he&nbsp;
also has his own leadership aspirations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.560
functionally takes control of the government&nbsp;
after Luna's death. He's shown to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:04.560
far more brutal than Silvio, but he gets karmic&nbsp;
payback and a half for everything he's done.&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.840
When word reaches him that Task Force X is at&nbsp;
Jotunheim, he personally leads the army assaulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:15.200
the tower. He and his men survive the explosion,&nbsp;
but they don't survive Starro. Finally free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.720
Starro kills or mind controls almost the entire&nbsp;
Corto Maltese military — including Suarez,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:25.600
who learns the hard way not to mess with&nbsp;
intergalactic starfish. He dies like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:31.680
rest of his men — confused and despondent as the&nbsp;
secret his government hid stops being a secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.011
in the least subtle way possible.

00:06:34.011 --> 00:06:40.240
Starro: an intergalactic starfish, and also&nbsp;
perhaps the most dangerous evil force in the DCEU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.640
Captured by astronauts who only found out about&nbsp;
his mind control ability after he spit some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.560
offspring at them, Starro was brought&nbsp;
to Earth for research. Understandably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.760
being kept in captivity for decades leaves him&nbsp;
with something of a grudge against humanity -- on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:07:00.000
top of his already existing conquering urges.
Long kept a secret by both the U.S. and Corto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:06.080
Maltese governments, Starro makes himself known to&nbsp;
the world in a big way after finally escaping from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:11.520
Jotunheim. After a thrilling battle, during which&nbsp;
Bloodsport finally shows his mettle as a leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:17.280
Starro is defeated. His eye is pierced by&nbsp;
Harley as rats jump in and tear his innards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:23.840
to shreds. Task Force X become heroes, and&nbsp;
end up in the rarest of all situations:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.000
having leverage over Amanda Waller.
 
